# The composed layers that owe no sum, and for which quantity. One row per (composed layer,
# ground), carrying which ground it was and which quantity it lifts.
#
# Read `quantity` before you use this
#   NULL means every quantity is suspended (nobody searched at all, a conversion nobody sized, or a
#   part that is not here); a value means exactly that quantity is (the overlap was found and could
#   not be sized against it, a layer the sum reads leaves it unstated, or a derived part cannot be
#   computed). A caller computing one quantity's sum wants `quantity IS NULL OR quantity = '<that
#   one>'`. Keeping only `demand` in the second branch and dropping the column would make "no sum at
#   all" true of one branch and false of the other, with nothing downstream able to tell.
#
# Why it exists: one name for what is excluded
#   Without it, the condition reaches its call sites as two negations joined by AND, in two
#   different SQL idioms: an `IS DISTINCT FROM` beside a `NOT EXISTS`. That is the shape most
#   likely to drift, two ways to say NOT, neither of which names what is being excluded. Not this
#   and not that is the same as not either, so the thing to name is the either: the fusions that
#   are suspended. The callers then anti-join one relation, and adding a ground for suspension is a
#   new file rather than a new clause in two conditions.
#
# Two kinds of ground, one set
#   composition/filed_grounds.sqlc holds the grounds read off what a fusion and its parts file;
#   composition/underived_parts.sqlc holds the one that needs a computation, a part filed `derived`
#   whose figure cannot be worked out. Every sum reads both, through this relation. Only
#   composition/suspended_remainders.sqlc reads the first alone, and its header says why.
#
# Run this on its own before reading any composition check
#   Every row here is a fusion whose arithmetic is not owed, and a check reporting a pass on one of
#   these rows is reporting about nothing.
#
# Repeated rows, on purpose
#   This repeats a fusion once per ground, which is why it is two files. One fusion can be suspended
#   on more than one ground (`every-unsized-conversion/shift-capacity` files both an unsearched
#   elimination and an unsized conversion), and it is suspended once. The key here is (composition,
#   composed_layer, quantity, suspended_because); the key a caller almost always wants is the first
#   three, and `composition/suspended_fusions.sqlc` keeps only those.
#
# The difference is invisible to almost every caller
#   That is why it needs saying. `EXISTS`, `NOT EXISTS` and `EXCEPT` cannot see repeats: a repeated
#   row changes nothing they compute. A `LEFT JOIN` can, and so can a law that counts fusions. So
#   the reduced relation is what a count or a join reads, and this file is what a reader who needs
#   the reason reads. A relation nobody has joined on has repeats nobody has checked.
-- the grounds that lift the sum rule: those read off the filing, and a derived part that cannot
-- be computed.
:union(ALL composition/filed_grounds.sqlc, composition/underived_parts.sqlc)
